---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Now from "@/components/Now.astro";
---

<Layout title="About - Pavel Kleisner">

    <section class="page-intro with-photo">
        <div>
            <h1>About me</h1>
            <p class="lead">A calm, practical engineer who still genuinely enjoys making and fixing things.</p>
            <div class="body">
                <p>I've worked with DataLite since 2017 and grew there from junior through senior engineer to team lead. For the last several years I've been a self-employed contractor on their client projects. I'm at my best where correctness, compatibility and release safety matter, where the code meets domain rules, integration contracts and production.</p>
                <p>I'd rather fix production than win an argument about it, and I expect the same from the people around me: real effort to get the work done and to keep learning.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="portrait">
            <img src="/pavel.jpg" alt="Pavel Kleisner" width="600" height="750" />
        </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>How I think about software</h2>
        <div class="tiles">
            <div class="tile"><h3>Small, explicit changes</h3><p>In a live system a precisely named rule, an isolated mapping or one focused test usually beats a big rewrite.</p></div>
            <div class="tile"><h3>Modern when it pays off</h3><p>New versions and tools when they make the system more reliable, more observable or easier to change. Not for the CV.</p></div>
            <div class="tile"><h3>Not done at deploy</h3><p>The real system starts after deployment: old data, retries, external contracts, logs, metrics and whoever has to support it.</p></div>
            <div class="tile"><h3>AI as a power tool</h3><p>Agents do as much as they reasonably can. The understanding, the review and the responsibility for what ships stay with me.</p></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="feature bleed">
        <div>
            <h2>What I deliberately avoid</h2>
            <p class="quote">A system isn't well designed if only one person can safely change it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
            <p><b>Over-engineering.</b> Architecture that doesn't remove real complexity, risk or maintenance cost. The "flexible" abstraction is usually the code everyone is afraid to touch two years later.</p>
            <p><b>Big-bang releases.</b> A rollout needs an order, a fallback and a way to stop halfway, otherwise it's a bet.</p>
            <p><b>One-person architecture.</b> I've been that person. It doesn't scale and it ruins holidays.</p>
            <p><b>Ego-driven choices.</b> I once switched our side back to a partner's old format rather than defend a technically correct spec.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Outside work</h2>
        <div class="prose">
            <p>I'm from the Krkonoše foothills and live just outside Prague with my family. I read a lot, mostly as audiobooks (LitRPG and Czech sci-fi), listen to metal and grind my coffee by hand.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="margin-top:1.5rem"><Now lang="en" /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta bleed">
        <h2>Sounds like someone you'd like to work with?</h2>
        <a class="email" href="mailto:contact@pavelkleisner.dev">contact@pavelkleisner.dev</a>
    </section>

</Layout>
